Once the energy levels of the quantum dots are aligned, we perform a stability diagram measurement by shifting the energy levels of all quantum dots to investigate the electron occupation of the array. The stability diagram allows us to determine the electron occupation of the array as a function of the energy levels of the quantum dots. At zero source–drain bias (dashed white line in Fig. 2b), there is only enough energy to add a single electron to the array at a time. In this regime, we simulate the SSH model in the two devices. Figure 3 shows a comparison of the experimental and theoretical results of the two devices. Figure 3a shows the calculated normalized zero-bias conductance as a function of the ratio of tunnel couplings (v/w) of the array. The design of device I in the trivial phase is given by the dashed red line \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
